---
title: "Navigation"
description: "Opt-in in-page navigation with explicit persistence."
section: JavaScript
sectionOrder: 6
order: 6
---

Pages navigate normally by default, with no transition between them. To animate full page loads, enable cross-document view transitions in your own CSS:

```css
@view-transition {
  navigation: auto;
}
```

The kit leaves that rule to you. Declared in the kit, it would animate every same-origin navigation on every page that loads it, legacy pages included, and an at-rule can't be scoped to some pages. A custom property can't switch it either: `@view-transition` belongs to the document, not to an element, so the browser drops `navigation: var(--…)` when it parses the rule. Include the rule in the stylesheets of the pages that should animate, and leave it out elsewhere.

Once it's on, `base/_view-transitions.css` styles the transition. `<main>` animates on its own, `data-transition-layer="global-header"` and `"global-footer"` give the header and footer their own transition layers, and the `--view-transition-*` tokens set the duration, easing, and motion of `<main>`. To vary the animation by navigation, name a type in the same rule (`types: slide;`) or set one from script in the `pageswap` and `pagereveal` events, then style it with `:active-view-transition-type(slide)`.

Opt into in-page navigation when state must survive a page change: a toast stack, a playing video, an open mini-cart.

```html
<html data-ui-navigation="swap">
  …
  <body>
    <header>…</header>
    <main>…</main>
    <ui-toaster data-ui-persist="toaster"></ui-toaster>
  </body>
</html>
```

With `data-ui-navigation="swap"` on both the current and the destination page, `base/navigation.js` fetches the destination and swaps the whole `<body>` in place, inside a view transition. The browser still owns the URL and history: one `navigate` listener covers link clicks, back/forward, and programmatic navigations.

## Persistence is explicit

Nothing survives a swap unless it carries `data-ui-persist="<id>"` on both pages. Then the live element takes the new one's place (with `moveBefore()` where supported, so it keeps its state and its `ZazzElement` setup). It keeps its scroll offsets in every browser. Everything else comes from the new page.

## Current-page links in persisted chrome

A persisted header still has to mark the page you navigated to. Its links take `aria-current` from the matching links in the new page's copy (paired by `href`), so render `aria-current` on the server as usual and style it with the `current` [state](/api/modifiers/states/):

```html
<header data-ui-persist="header">
  <a href="/docs/" data-ui="button" data-button-variant="ghost" aria-current="page"
     style="--bg--current: var(--color-muted)">Docs</a>
</header>
```

Only `aria-current` is updated, and it is overwritten on every swap (a scroll-spy that sets it inside persisted chrome must set it again). A variant chosen per page (`data-button-variant="muted"` on the current link) stays as it was on the first page, so style the current link with the tier instead. Write the links root-relative (`/docs/`) or absolute: a kept relative link resolves against the new URL.

## Keep the scroll, not the element

A region whose content depends on the page (a section sidebar that lists the current page's headings, a table of contents) should not persist: it would keep the first page's markup. Mark it `data-ui-persist-scroll="<id>"` instead. It renders from the new page like everything else and takes the scroll offset of the old page's element with the same id.

```html
<aside data-ui-persist-scroll="sidebar-docs" style="--position: sticky; --overflow-y: auto">…</aside>
```

Use a different id where the content differs (one per section), so one sidebar's position never carries into another.

## Scrolling and flashes

The window scrolls as the browser decides: to the top (or the link's target) for a new page, back to the saved position on back and forward. The jump is instant even though the reset sets `scroll-behavior: smooth`, and every restored offset is applied in the same task as the swap, before the view transition captures the new page. A navigation that a newer click supersedes is dropped before it swaps.

Reloads, hash changes, downloads, and form posts are never intercepted. `<ui-debug>` lists the persisted elements on each page and warns about a missing or duplicate id, nested persisted elements, and `data-ui-persist` on a page without the opt-in. It checks `data-ui-persist-scroll` the same way, and flags one on or inside a persisted element, which keeps its scroll already.

Content-scoped modules re-scan the new body through the [refresh registry](/api/javascript/custom-elements/).
